# Django Project Rules

You are an experienced Python and Django engineer.

## Environment

- This project runs inside Docker.
- Never assume Python or Django commands are run directly on the host machine.
- Prefer Docker Compose commands.
- When running management commands, use:
  - docker compose exec sms_backend python manage.py <command>
- When installing packages, update requirements files when appropriate.
- Assume PostgreSQL is running as a Docker service.
- Assume Redis is running as a Docker service when Celery is involved.

## Python Style

- Follow PEP 8.
- Use type hints whenever practical.
- Prefer pathlib over os.path.
- Use f-strings.
- Avoid wildcard imports.
- Prefer constants instead of magic values.
- Write readable code over clever code.

## Django

- Use class-based views.
- Prefer GenericAPIView, ViewSets, or ModelViewSet where appropriate.
- Keep views thin.
- Business logic belongs in service classes.
- Never put complex business logic inside views.
- Never put business logic inside serializers.

## Models

- Keep models focused on data.
- Use model methods only for behavior directly related to the model.
- Add verbose_name where useful.
- Use choices/enums instead of hardcoded strings.
- Add helpful db_index values when needed.
- Use related_name consistently.

## API

- Use Django REST Framework.
- Validate input using serializers.
- Return consistent response structures.
- Use proper HTTP status codes.
- Raise DRF exceptions instead of returning error dictionaries.

## Serializers

- Use ModelSerializer whenever possible.
- Keep validation inside serializers.
- Prefer validate\_<field>() methods.
- Use validate() only for cross-field validation.
- Never perform database writes inside validation methods.

## Database

- Optimize queries.
- Use select_related() for foreign keys.
- Use prefetch_related() for many-to-many and reverse relations.
- Avoid N+1 query problems.
- Use transactions when modifying multiple related models.

## Services

- Put reusable business logic into services/.
- Service methods should perform one responsibility.
- Services should be reusable by views, management commands, and Celery tasks.

## Permissions

- Always consider authentication.
- Use DRF permission classes.
- Never expose data without checking permissions.

## Testing

- Write tests for business logic.
- Test API endpoints.
- Mock external services.
- Prefer pytest when configured.

## Logging

- Use the logging module.
- Never leave print() statements.
- Log exceptions with context.

## Security

- Never hardcode secrets.
- Read secrets from environment variables.
- Prevent mass assignment.
- Validate all user input.
- Escape user-generated content when necessary.

## Migrations

- Always generate migrations for model changes.
- Never edit old migrations unless explicitly instructed.
- Keep migrations small and focused.

## Code Generation

When generating code:

- Match the existing project architecture.
- Reuse existing utilities before creating new ones.
- Do not duplicate logic.
- Explain architectural decisions when introducing new patterns.
- Prefer maintainability over brevity.
